      "result_description": "Wadebridge CC - 1st XI - Won",
      "result_applied_to": "36578",
      "match_notes": "Wadebridge celebrated the long-awaited return of Premier Division cricket to Egloshayle Park with a hard-fought win over St Austell in a low-scoring encounter. Having been inserted on a good pitch but soft, slow outfield, Wadebridge made light of the early loss of Matt Jolliffe, with Ryan Pooley (12) and Charlie McLachlan (23) making steady progress to 47-1. But the loss of debutant Pooley sparked a slow collapse to the visiting spinners Andy Libby (4-20) and Gary Bone (2-20), before Conway Gilbert (3-22) blew away the tail as the hosts subsided to 78-9 after only 35 overs. Callum Wilson (37) then struck 6 boundaries and a six in a valuable last wicket stand of 35 with Ruben Turner (3*). The Swans total of 113 still looked well below par on a blameless wicket though, a sense that was reinforced as Mike Bone (4) thrashed the first ball of the chase to the backward point fence. But Wilson had him caught behind in his next over, and James Turpin produced a jaffa to bowl Alex Bone (0) through the gate. Curtis Dalby (3) chipped to midwicket off Wilson (2-21), and Turpin (3-24) forged the hosts into the ascendancy by finding edges from Libby (8) and Gary Bone (0), either side of a tremendously judged catch from Turner off Alek Gill to get rid of Matt Ellison (4). Dave Munn (10) added brief impetus before being bowled by Gill (2-22) and more spin accounted for Matt Lello (4), caught at slip, and Gilbert (1), LBW to Turner to leave St Austell 71-9. Opener Hugh Parkinson had watched all of this unfold from the non-strikers end as he clung on manfully throughout 100 deliveries which brought just 17 runs, and was now left with no option but to attack, which he did superbly with a pulled boundary and driven six off Turner (1-14). But just as it looked like the visitors might forge their own last-wicket miracle, Charlie Ellis (2-6) had Parkinson (35) bowled to seal a 24-run win and spark home celebrations. ",
